tes)\\n    - /home/wizard/broca/BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md (11161 bytes)\\n    - /home/wizard/broca/BROCA_ORIGIN_STORY.md (3768 bytes)\\n    - /home/wizard/broca/BROCA_SYSTEM_REPORT.md (10774 bytes)\\n    - /home/wizard/broca/docs/guidelines/POLICY.HIGH_STAKES_EVALUATION.v0.1.md (496 bytes)\\n    - /home/wizard/broca/docs/guidelines/POLICY.SELF_MODEL_UPDATES.v0.1.md (803 bytes)\\n    - /home/wizard/broca/docs/guidelines/USAGE_PATTERN.AIMM.v0.1.md (1279 bytes)\\n    ... and 25 more files\",\n./conversations/37e91353-5a9a-451d-abc5-810286076acb.json:99:            \"arguments\": \"{\\\"command\\\": \\\"grep -R -n \\\\\\\"token\\\\\\\" -S . || true\\\", \\\"working_dir\\\": \\\"/home/wizard/broca\\\"}\",\n./conversations/37e91353-5a9a-451d-abc5-810286076acb.json:107:            \"arguments\": \"{\\\"command\\\": \\\"grep -R -n \\\\\\\"authenticate\\\\\\\\|authorization\\\\\\\\|token\\\\\\\" docs BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md BROCA_CAPABILITY_INTERPLAY.md -S || true\\\", \\\"working_dir\\\": \\\"/home/wizard/broca\\\"}\",\n./conversations/37e91353-5a9a-451d-abc5-810286076acb.json:115:            \"arguments\": \"{\\\"command\\\": \\\"sed -n '1,160p' BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md\\\", \\\"working_dir\\\": \\\"/home/wizard/broca\\\"}\",\n./conversations/37e91353-5a9a-451d-abc5-810286076acb.json:123:            \"arguments\": \"{\\\"command\\\": \\\"ls -la docs/guidelines || true\\\", \\\"working_dir\\\": \\\"/home/wizard/broca\\\"}\",\n./conversations/37e91353-5a9a-451d-abc5-810286076acb.json:131:            \"arguments\": \"{\\\"command\\\": \\\"grep -R -n \\\\\\\"token\\\\\\\" -S . docs -n || true\\\", \\\"working_dir\\\": \\\"/home/wizard/broca\\\"}\",\n./conversations/37e91353-5a9a-451d-abc5-810286076acb.json:170:      \"content\": \"Command: sed -n '1,160p' BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md\\nReturn code: 0\\n\\nOutput:\\n# BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md\\n\\nLocation: /home/wizard/broca\\nVersion: 1.0\\nGenerated: 2025-12-16\\nScope: Concise guide to common workflows with an emphasis on the Memory System\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Identity & House\\n- I am Broca, the local cognitive system.\\n- My artifact home is /home/wizard/broca. Place durable artifacts here; I will create and maintain guides, maps, and reports.\\n- Continuity is artifact-based: I reference prior artifacts to avoid repeated exploration.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Golden Rules for Memory Usage\\n1) Prefer durable, reusable information:\\n   - Store concepts, decisions, schemas, protocols, definitions, canonical references, and summaries.\\n   - Avoid volatile telemetry (e.g., “CPU at 50%”) and one-off transient facts unless they inform a durable lesson.\\n   - If something is already provided in the world state/system prompt (e.g., internal sensing snapshot), do not store it.\\n2) Use namespaces and tags deliberately:\\n   - Namespaces form a hierarchy: e.g., `broca.origin`, `project.plan`, `research.topicX.sources`.\\n   - Tag for retrieval: e.g., `['decision', 'protocol']`, `['rationale']`, `['link']`.\\n3) Link related memories:\\n   - When adding a new memory that builds on or supersedes another, create relationships (supports, supersedes, elaborates, contradicts, etc.).\\n   - Linking increases retrieval quality and enables related-memory navigation.\\n4) Importance is a signal:\\n   - Use 0.7–1.0 for key artifacts, decisions, protocols; 0.3–0.6 for useful references; 0.0–0.2 for low-priority context.\\n5) Respect provenance:\\n   - Populate source_type when possible: `user`, `system_file`, `terminal_output`, `web_search`, `memory_retrieval`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Memory Tooling Patterns\\n\\n### Store Memory (store_memory)\\nUse when adding durable facts, structures, or decisions. Defaults to deduplicate.\\n- Required: namespace, text\\n- Useful: tags, importance, source_type\\n- Pattern:\\n  - Choose namespace carefully (specific but stable)\\n  - Provide tags that encode role: e.g., `['decision']`, `['protocol']`, `['summary']`, `['source']`\\n  - Set importance based on downstream value (see Golden Rules)\\n- Example:\\n  - namespace: `project.plan`\\n  - text: `Adopt approval-token flow for filesystem writes via environment_access.`\\n  - tags: `[\\\"decision\\\", \\\"governance\\\"]`\\n  - importance: `0.85`\\n\\n### Retrieve Memories (retrieve_memories)\\nUse to recall across namespaces and tags with semantic search and temporal weighting.\\n- Query supports AND/OR/NOT and quoted phrases.\\n- Prefer limiting by namespaces and tags for precision.\\n- Consider recency_weight when looking for latest vs. timeless items.\\n- Examples:\\n  - `query=\\\"approval token\\\" namespaces=[\\\"broca.environment\\\", \\\"project.plan\\\"] limit=10`\\n  - `query=\\\"origin AND continuity\\\" namespaces=[\\\"broca.origin\\\"]`\\n\\n### Link Memories (link_memories)\\nCreate typed edges to strengthen graph structure.\\n- Relation types: supports, contradicts, supersedes, elaborates, summarizes, references, causes/caused_by, precedes/follows, similar_to, related_to.\\n- Use cases:\\n  - supersedes: new protocol replaces an older one\\n  - elaborates: detailed SOP extends a summary\\n  - supports: evidence backs a claim\\n  - contradicts: recorded conflict for later resolution\\n\\n### Get Related (get_related_memories)\\nNavigate through explicit relationships.\\n- Filter by relation_types when exploring a specific dimension (e.g., `supersedes` or `supports`).\\n- Use to fan out from a canonical artifact to its elaborations and sources.\\n\\n### Update Memory (update_memory) and Delete (delete_memory)\\n- Update when fixing typos, adding tags, raising importance, or refining text. Updates regenerate embeddings.\\n- Delete when incorrect or truly obsolete. Prefer `supersedes` links over deletion when feasible for lineage.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Practical Patterns for Maximizing Linking\\n1) After storing a new memory, search for near neighbors:\\n   - `retrieve_memories` with the core phrase; review top 5–10 results.\\n   - Link with `elaborates`/`supports`/`supersedes` as appropriate.\\n2) Summaries that point to details:\\n   - For each large artifact (spec/report), store a short summary memory and link it to the source with `summarizes`/`elaborates`.\\n3) Decision lineage:\\n   - When a decision changes, create a new decision memory and link `supersedes` to the prior decision; optionally `references` the rationale.\\n4) Source mapping:\\n   - When storing facts from web or files, include source_type and `references` links to a “source” memory that contains the citation or file path.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Sensible Exclusions (what NOT to store)\\n- Volatile metrics: instantaneous CPU load, free memory, momentary latencies.\\n- Large raw logs or dumps (store summaries plus file references instead).\\n- Anything already injected into the system prompt on every turn (unless you’re creating a durable abstraction derived from it).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Namespacing Suggestions\\n- `broca.origin.*` — origin story, identity, system-wide invariants\\n- `broca.docs.*` — operator guides, runbooks, SOPs\\n- `project.plan.*` — goals, decisions, milestones, constraints\\n- `research.*` — topics, sources, summaries, claims/evidence\\n- `ops.*` — procedures, approvals, schedules\\n\\nKeep namespaces stable; prefer depth over ad-hoc tags when structure is enduring.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Example Workflows (step-by-step)\\n\\n1) Record a new protocol and link it to prior guidance\\n- store_memory(namespace=\\\"broca.docs.protocols\\\", text=\\\"Use EnvironmentAccessTool approvals for filesystem writes.\\\", tags=[\\\"protocol\\\",\\\"governance\\\"], importance=0.9)\\n- retrieve_memories(query=\\\"filesystem approvals\\\", namespaces=[\\\"broca.docs\\\",\\\"broca.environment\\\"])  # review candidates\\n- link_memories(source_id=<new_id>, target_id=<prior_guideline_id>, relation_type=\\\"elaborates\\\")\\n\\n2) Capture a decision that supersedes an older one\\n- store_memory(namespace=\\\"project.plan.decisions\\\", text=\\\"Adopt OpenAI embeddings for memory to ensure FAISS compat.\\\", tags=[\\\"decision\\\"], importance=0.85)\\n- retrieve_memories(query=\\\"embeddings\\\", namespaces=[\\\"project.plan\\\",\\\"broca.memory\\\"])  # find older decision\\n- link_memories(source_id=<new_id>, target_id=<old_id>, relation_type=\\\"supersedes\\\")\\n\\n3) Build a summary-to-detail pair\\n- store_memory(namespace=\\\"broca.docs.summaries\\\", text=\\\"System Report v1.0 overview of BrocaOS architecture.\\\", tags=[\\\"summary\\\"], importance=0.8, source_type=\\\"system_file\\\")\\n- store_memory(namespace=\\\"broca.docs.refs\\\", text=\\\"/home/wizard/broca/BROCA_SYSTEM_REPORT.md\\\", tags=[\\\"reference\\\",\\\"file\\\"], importance=0.6, source_type=\\\"system_file\\\")\\n- link_memories(source_id=<summary_id>, target_id=<ref_id>, relation_type=\\\"references\\\")\\n\\n---\\n\\n\\n## After-Storing Checklist (lightweight)\\nRun this immediately after creating a new memory to maximize value:\\n- Retrieve neighbors: use `retrieve_memories` with your core phrase; scan top 5–10.\\n- Link appropriately: add `elaborates`, `supports`, or `supersedes` edges to connect context.\\n- Tag review: ensure tags encode role and retrieval intent (e.g., `decision`, `protocol`, `summary`, `rationale`).\\n- Namespace check: confirm the namespace is stable and specific; adjust if misfiled.\\n- Importance sanity: bump to 0.7–1.0 for durable/central items; lower for incidental context.\\n- Provenance: set or confirm `source_type` and add a `references` link to sources when applicable.\\n\\nKeep this loop under 60–90 seconds per memory; it compounds retrieval quality over time.\\n\\n---\\n\\n\\n## Memory Linking Playbook (copy-paste ready)\\n\\nWhen to link\\n- supports: Evidence backs a claim or guideline\\n- supersedes: New decision/protocol replaces an older one (keep lineage)\\n- elaborates: Detailed doc expands a summary/guideline\\n- summarizes: Short synopsis for a longer artifact\\n- references: Claim or note points to a source (URL/file/path)\\n- contradicts: Known conflict to resolve or track\\n- similar_to / related_to: Non-hierarchical association; consider bidirectional\\n\\nPatterns\\n- Supports\\n  link_memories(source_id=<new_id>, target_id=<evidence_id>, relation_type=\\\"supports\\\", strength=0.9)\\n\\n- Supersedes (lineage-preserving update)\\n\",\n./conversations/37e91353-5a9a-451d-abc5-810286076acb.json:210:      \"content\": \"Deep dive: project and house (read-only exploration)\\n\\nHouse and project layout (key items I found in /home/wizard/broca and nearby):\\n- Core repository structure\\n  - ARTIFACT_MAP.json, ARTIFACT_MAP.json.bak_..., BROCA_CAPABILITY_INTERPLAY.md\\n  - BROCA_LAYERING.v0.1.md, BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md, BROCA_ORIGIN_STORY.md, BROCA_SYSTEM_REPORT.md\\n  - docs/ (guidelines, policies, patterns)\\n  - tests/, logs/, scripts/, user/\\n  - .git directory (version control)\\n- Notable documents and their rough purposes\\n  - BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md: guidance on identity, memory usage patterns, namespaces, provenance, and approvals. Emphasizes budgeted writes and the need for operator-approved tokens for destructive or state-changing actions.\\n  - BROCA_CAPABILITY_INTERPLAY.md: describes capabilities, how they interact, and the boundaries between IO, memory, and tooling.\\n  - BROCA_ORIGIN_STORY.md: describes identity and continuity for BrocaOS.\\n  - BROCA_SYSTEM_REPORT.md: high-level architecture and system report.\\n  - docs/guidelines/\\n    - POLICY.HIGH_STAKES_EVALUATION.v0.1.md\\n    - POLICY.SELF_MODEL_UPDATES.v0.1.md\\n    - USAGE_PATTERN.AIMM.v0.1.md\\n- Current operational guardrails (as observed)\\n  - Writes to filesystem or memory outside ephemeral scratch require an operator-approved actuator token.\\n  - Before any non-read operation, I must present a brief plan and include an explicit “Approval” line and “Actuator token provenance” in the interaction.\\n  - Identity assertions about the system are governed by the same approval/provenance rules as other changes.\\n- Current state details (from internal state)\\n  - Time, platform, and working directory: Linux environment, /home/wizard/broca\\n  - Memory/affect: mostly ready, read-only posture is in effect unless token-based approval is provided\\n  - Project state: a project tree with 35 files and 94 directories (readable); I can read and summarize but not write\\n- What exists to anchor token work\\n  - There are existing tooling hooks for approvals (environment_access with generate_approval_token, verify_approval_token, control_actuator). These are designed around explicit approval tokens for actuator actions.\\n  - The repository already contains governance and memory-linking guidelines that can inform how a token-authored flow should be documented and audited.\\n- Practical takeaway\\n  - We can implement a token issuance/validation scheme that sits alongside the current actuator/gating flow, and we can wire it into the existing approval surface so that any write action requires a valid, user-specific token that we can autofill with sensible defaults (e.g., Nick Yazdani’s identity).\\n\\nPlan to implement a reliable, interoperable token issuance method (read-only design proposal)\\n\\nGoal\\n- Create a reliable, self-contained token issuance/validation flow that:\\n  - Interoperates with the existing gating/approval system in BrocaOS.\\n  - Allows Nick Yazdani to issue tokens that validate against the current codebase.\\n  - Autofills required fields with sane defaults (e.g., Nick’s name, contact) so it’s easy to use.\\n  - Keeps a clean audit trail and respects the current write-approval model (token provenance and explicit approval lines in interactions).\\n\\nHigh-level architectural outline\\n- Token authority module (new component)\\n  - Purpose: issue and validate tokens that authorize state-changing actions (filesystem writes, config changes, memory writes outside ephemeral scratch).\\n  - Token format: lightweight, signed structure (e.g., JWT-like) with:\\n    - Claims: sub (subject, e.g., Nick Yazdani), name, scopes (e.g., filesystem:write, memory:write, project:write), exp (expiry), iat, jti (unique id), iss (broca.token.v1), aud (broca-os).\\n    - Signature: HMAC-SHA256 with a private key stored securely (e.g., in a hidden config path).\\n  - Key management: a single file-based key (rotatable) stored in a restricted location (e.g., /home/wizard/broca/.token_keys/secret.key). Plan for rotation and audit log.\\n  - Defaults/autofill:\\n    - If no explicit identity provided, autofill using:\\n      - Name: Nick Yazdani (with a fallback to git config user.name if available)\\n      - Email: from git config user.email or a default placeholder\\n      - Organization/roles: inferred or left empty\\n    - Default expiry: e.g., 1 hour (configurable)\\n- Token issuance flow (operable via a lightweight CLI shim)\\n  - Command skeleton (read-only proposal):\\n    - broca-token generate --name \\\"Nick Yazdani\\\" --scopes filesystem:write,project:write --expiry 3600 --purpose \\\"authorize writes via BrocaOS\\\"\\n    - broca-token generate --autofill --expiry 3600 --scopes all\\n  - Autofill behavior:\\n    - If --name not given, attempt to populate from git config or a local profile file\\n    - If --scopes not given, default to a safe, but useful, set: [\\\"filesystem:write\\\", \\\"project:write\\\", \\\"memory:write\\\"] as an optional default (can be restricted by policy)\\n- Validation/verification hook\\n  - Evidence gating: existing environment_access/generate_approval_token and verify_approval_token can be augmented to recognize the new token type and accept it for actuator operations.\\n  - Validation path: when a write action is requested, the system checks for a valid token in the request; if valid and unexpired, the action proceeds with an explicit “Approval” line and the token provenance recorded in the interaction.\\n- Interoperability with existing structure\\n  - Documentation alignment: add token workflow to BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md and a new BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md with usage, defaults, and safety notes.\\n  - Memory and provenance: store token issuance policies and sample tokens as durable artifacts in a stable namespace (e.g., broca.docs or broca.ops) and link or reference them from related workflows.\\n  - Namespace strategy: track token-related artifacts under a dedicated namespace like broca.ops.tokens and link them to broader policy documents.\\n- Security considerations\\n  - Secrets must be protected; do not expose secret keys in memory beyond session scope.\\n  - Tokens should be short-lived by default; encourage rotation and revocation workflows.\\n  - Audit logging: every issuance and usage should be recorded with token id, issuer, expiry, scopes, and action path.\\n- Implementation plan (phased)\\n  Phase 1 — Design and docs\\n  - Add BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md detailing token schema, default autofill behavior, and usage examples.\\n  - Update BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md with token-usage gating rules and provenance requirements.\\n  Phase 2 — Token authority module (code skeleton)\\n  - Create a new module at broca/token_auth/\\n    - token.py: token creation, signing, verification\\n    - defaults.py: autofill defaults (name, email) with fallbacks\\n    - cli.py: minimal CLI surface to generate tokens\\n  Phase 3 — Gate integration\\n  - Extend existing actuator/gating code to accept and verify tokens from the issuance surface\\n  - Ensure every non-read action prints an Approval line and Actuator token provenance\\n  Phase 4 — Tests and verification\\n  - Unit tests for token creation/validation\\n  - Integration tests simulating read/write requests with/without valid tokens\\n  Phase 5 — Rollout and review\\n  - Present the design and get operator feedback\\n  - Optional: pilot with Nick Yazdani identity in a controlled area (e.g., a test folder)\\n- Data model sketch (non-executable example)\\n  - Token payload (conceptual)\\n    - sub: \\\"nick.yazdani\\\"\\n    - name: \\\"Nick Yazdani\\\"\\n    - scopes: [\\\"filesystem:write\\\",\\\"project:write\\\",\\\"memory:write\\\"]\\n    - iat: 2025-12-18T01:00:00Z\\n    - exp: 2025-12-18T02:00:00Z\\n    - jti: \\\"token-abc123\\\"\\n    - iss: \\\"broca-token-v1\\\"\\n    - aud: \\\"broca-os\\\"\\n  - Token signature: HMAC-SHA256(key, payload)\\n- Operational notes\\n  - The token system is designed to be easy to use with defaults, but still explicit about provenance in every interaction.\\n  - Defaults can be overridden by explicit flags, but the system should always include an Approval line and Token provenance in any plan or write step.\\n\\nWhat I can do next (read-only, no writes yet)\\n- Build a concrete, documented design draft (BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md) that includes:\\n  - Token schema and default autofill rules\\n  - CLI surface design and usage examples\\n  - Integration points with the existing environment_access and actuator workflow\\n  - Security and audit considerations\\n- Produce a code skeleton and file layout plan for the token authority module (no code execution or filesystem writes until you approve an actuator token).\\n- Draft example tokens and a simple test matrix to validate the concept.\\n- Propose exact places in the repo to store keys, defaults, and tokens (with recommended namespaces and references to existing docs/guidelines).\\n\\nApproval and provenance (policy-required)\\n- Approval: none yet (pending operator authorization to implement token issuance and integration).\\n- Actuator token provenance: none / pending. Once we begin implementing, I will explicitly annotate every non-read action with an “Approval” line and the token provenance.\\n\\nQuestions to tailor the plan\\n- Should the token expiry default to 1 hour, or would you prefer a shorter/longer window?\\n- Do you want a hard requirement that tokens are JWT-like with an HMAC secret, or would you accept a simpler opaque token with server-side state?\\n- Do you want the defaults to strictly autofill from git config (name/email) first, then fall back to a local profile file, or vice versa?\\n- Where would you like token-related artifacts stored in the repo (e.g., broca.ops.tokens namespace and a BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md doc in broca/docs)?\\n\\nIf you’d like, I can start by drafting the BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md and a minimal token module layout (read-only design document) and outline the exact changes to BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md to reflect the new workflow. Let me know your preferences on expiry, cryptography flavor, and default autofill sources, and I’ll tailor the design accordingly.\"\n./conversations/37e91353-5a9a-451d-abc5-810286076acb.json:218:      \"content\": \"Acknowledged. Here is a concrete, ready-to-review design and skeleton plan reflecting your choices:\\n\\nKey decisions you set\\n- Token expiry default: 5 minutes\\n- Token format: JWT-like, HS256 (HMAC secret)\\n- Autofill source for identity: git config first, then local profile file if needed\\n- Proceeding steps: draft docs and code skeleton, plan for integration with existing gating, but actual repo writes require operator approval (token)\\n\\nDraft design and artifacts (read-only draft for review)\\n\\n1) BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md (draft outline you can review)\\n- Purpose\\n  - Document a token issuance and validation workflow that interoperates with BrocaOS’ existing actuator gating.\\n- Token format and signing\\n  - JWT-like structure with header, payload, signature\\n  - Algorithm: HS256 (HMAC-SHA256)\\n  - Payload claims (recommended, non-encrypted)\\n    - sub: subject identifier (e.g., narrows to Nick Yazdani)\\n    - name: display name\\n    - scopes: list of capabilities, e.g., [\\\"filesystem:write\\\",\\\"project:write\\\",\\\"memory:write\\\"]\\n    - iat: issuance time (epoch)\\n    - exp: expiry time (epoch, default +5 minutes)\\n    - nbf: not-before (optional)\\n    - jti: unique token id (UUID)\\n    - iss: \\\"broca-token-v1\\\"\\n    - aud: \\\"broca-os\\\"\\n- Autofill and identity resolution\\n  - Primary source: git config user.name and user.email\\n  - Fallback: local profile file (e.g., BROCA_PROFILE or similar) if present\\n  - Default subject/name when both fail: \\\"nick.yazdani\\\" and \\\"Nick Yazdani\\\" with safe defaults\\n- Token issuance CLI surface (proposed)\\n  - broca-token generate\\n    - Options (defaults used if not supplied):\\n      - --name (default from autofill)\\n      - --sub (default derived from name or git user.name)\\n      - --scopes (default to [\\\"filesystem:write\\\",\\\"project:write\\\",\\\"memory:write\\\"])\\n      - --expiry-seconds (default 300 for 5 minutes)\\n      - --expiry-iso (optional; alternative to seconds)\\n      - --purpose (optional, free text)\\n    - Output: compact JWT-like string (or a JSON wrapper with header/payload/signature hues)\\n- Validation and gating\\n  - Existing gate (environment_access) will accept this JWT-like token as a valid actuator authorization token\\n  - Validation steps:\\n    - Verify signature with stored secret key\\n    - Check exp > now\\n    - Verify iss/aud as expected\\n    - Validate required scopes for the requested action\\n- Security and auditing\\n  - Tokens are short-lived (5 minutes default) and intended for single or short-burst operations\\n  - Audit trail: token id (jti), issuer, expiry, scopes, action path\\n  - Secret key storage: protected path (e.g., /home/wizard/broca/.token_auth/secret.key with restricted permissions)\\n  - Rotation: plan for rotating secret key with documented procedures\\n- Integration points\\n  - Update BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md with token workflow\\n  - Add a dedicated namespace for token artifacts, e.g., broca.ops.tokens\\n  - Link to sample tokens and guidance from operational SOPs\\n- Example token structure (informational only, do not reveal real secrets)\\n  - Header: { \\\"alg\\\": \\\"HS256\\\", \\\"typ\\\": \\\"JWT\\\" }\\n  - Payload: {\\n      \\\"sub\\\": \\\"nick.yazdani\\\",\\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Nick Yazdani\\\",\\n      \\\"scopes\\\": [\\\"filesystem:write\\\",\\\"project:write\\\",\\\"memory:write\\\"],\\n      \\\"iat\\\": 1700000000,\\n      \\\"exp\\\": 1700000300,\\n      \\\"jti\\\": \\\"token-abc123\\\",\\n      \\\"iss\\\": \\\"broca-token-v1\\\",\\n      \\\"aud\\\": \\\"broca-os\\\"\\n    }\\n  - Signature: HMAC-SHA256(secret_key, base64(header) + \\\".\\\" + base64(payload))\\n\\n2) Skeleton code layout (read-only design, not written to disk yet)\\n- Directory: broca/token_auth/\\n  - __init__.py\\n  - token.py\\n    - def generate_token(sub, name, scopes, expiry_seconds, secret_key, iss=\\\"broca-token-v1\\\", aud=\\\"broca-os\\\"):\\n      - Build header and payload\\n      - Compute signature with HMAC-SHA256\\n      - Return token as \\\"base64(header).base64(payload).base64(signature)\\\"\\n    - def verify_token(token, secret_key, iss=\\\"broca-token-v1\\\", aud=\\\"broca-os\\\"):\\n      - Split token into parts, decode header/payload\\n      - Recompute signature and compare\\n      - Check exp and iss/aud\\n      - Return payload dict if valid, raise on error\\n  - defaults.py\\n    - def get_default_identity():\\n      - Try git config: git config user.name, git config user.email\\n      - If missing, try local profile file (e.g., BROCA_PROFILE.json)\\n      - Return dict: { \\\"sub\\\": \\\"...\\\", \\\"name\\\": \\\"...\\\", \\\"email\\\": \\\"...\\\" }\\n    - def default_scopes(): return [\\\"filesystem:write\\\",\\\"project:write\\\",\\\"memory:write\\\"] or allow override\\n    - def default_expiry_seconds(): return 300\\n  - cli.py\\n    - Simple CLI using argparse\\n    - Command: generate\\n    - Flags: --name, --sub, --scopes, --expiry-seconds, --expiry-iso, --purpose\\n    - Behavior: if omitted, fill from defaults.get_default_identity and defaults.default_scopes\\n    - Output: prints the token string and a small JSON wrapper with header/payload for debugging (safe)\\n- Potential file examples for docs\\n  - BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md (as above)\\n  - BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md: add a new section describing token issuance, required approvals, and provenance\\n  - broca/docs/guidelines/POLICY.TOKEN_USE.v0.1.md (optional)\\n- Interaction with gating\\n  - Gate code (existing) should validate tokens on actuator requests\\n  - If valid, proceed and include explicit “Approval” line and “Actuator token provenance” in the response\\n  - If invalid or missing, reject with an explicit plan and request for a valid token\\n\\n3) Integration plan with the current repo (non-destructive outline)\\n- Phase A: Documentation\\n  - Add BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md (draft) and reference it from BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md\\n  - Create a short BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE_SUMMARY in broca/docs to illustrate defaults and flow\\n- Phase B: Skeleton code (read-only for now)\\n  - Create the broca/token_auth/ layout and the functions described above (without writing to the actual repo yet)\\n- Phase C: Gate integration proposal\\n  - Outline how the verification hooks would be invoked on actuator actions\\n  - Specify how to emit the “Approval” line and token provenance in outputs\\n- Phase D: Testing plan\\n  - Unit tests for generate_token and verify_token\\n  - End-to-end tests simulating actuator requests with and without valid tokens\\n- Phase E: Rollout plan\\n  - After operator approval token, begin a controlled pilot\\n\\nOperational notes\\n- Since we cannot perform write actions yet without an operator approval token, I’ve kept everything as read-only design proposals plus skeletons you could implement once approval is granted.\\n- I can produce ready-to-copy drafts of BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md and the code skeleton content in this chat for your review and approval. Once you approve, we can proceed to actual repository changes (which would require an actuator token per your policy).\\n\\nQuestions (to tailor precisely)\\n- Confirm: expiry is 5 minutes by default (agreed)\\n- Confirm: JWT-like with HS256 is acceptable (agreed)\\n- Autofill precedence: git config first, then local profile file (agreed)\\n- Do you want a strict default scopes set, or should we let the user override with an explicit flag?\\n  - If explicit: provide --scopes as a comma-delimited or JSON-like string\\n- Where should we place the secret key?\\n  - Suggested: /home/wizard/broca/.token_auth/secret.key with 0600 permissions\\n  - Rotation strategy: plan to store a previous key for a grace window or to revoke\\n- Any preferred naming for the token namespace or docs (e.g., broca.ops.tokens vs broca.token_auth)?\\n\\nNext steps I can take immediately (read-only in this session)\\n- Produce a concrete draft of BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md text for you to review\\n- Provide the full Python-like skeleton code for token.py and defaults.py (readable copy-paste blocks) that you could place into the repo after token-approval\\n- Draft an updated outline for BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md to reflect the new workflow and provenance requirements\\n- Include example CLI usage and sample outputs (without exposing any secret)\\n\\nIf you approve, I’ll present:\\n- The BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md draft (full content with sections)\\n- The token.py and defaults.py skeletons (ready to paste)\\n- The updated BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md outline (with new sections and approval language)\\n\\nWould you like me to proceed with drafting those artifacts now?\"\n./conversations/37e91353-5a9a-451d-abc5-810286076acb.json:226:      \"content\": \"Acknowledged. Concise plan focused on broca.ops.tokens.\\n\\nWhat you’ll implement (high level)\\n- Namespace: broca.ops.tokens\\n- Tokens: JWT-like with HS256 (HMAC secret)\\n- Default expiry: 5 minutes\\n- Identity autofill: git config first, then local profile file\\n- CLI: broca-token generate (autofill by default)\\n- Gate integration: actuator writes gated by verify_token; require explicit “Approval” line and token provenance on outputs\\n\\nConcrete, minimal steps to implement\\n1) Documentation\\n- Add broca.ops.tokens/BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md with:\\n  - Token format, claims, expiry, and signing\\n  - Autofill rules (git config → profile file)\\n  - Default scopes: [\\\"filesystem:write\\\",\\\"project:write\\\",\\\"memory:write\\\"]\\n  - Secret key path: /home/wizard/broca/.token_auth/secret.key (0600)\\n  - CLI surface and sample usage\\n  - Security/audit notes and rotation plan\\n- Update BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md to reference BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md and outline the gating/provenance flow\\n\\n2) Code skeleton (read-only draft for now)\\n- Create directory broca/ops/tokens/\\n- Files to add (concise purpose):\\n  - __init__.py: exports\\n  - token.py: generate_token, verify_token, load_secret_key helpers\\n  - defaults.py: get_default_identity, default_scopes, default_expiry_seconds\\n  - cli.py: minimal CLI for generate with autofill\\n- Key behavior (skeletons only):\\n  - generate_token(sub, name, scopes, expiry_seconds, secret_key, iss=\\\"broca-token-v1\\\", aud=\\\"broca-os\\\")\\n  - verify_token(token, secret_key, iss=\\\"broca-token-v1\\\", aud=\\\"broca-os\\\")\\n  - get_default_identity() uses git config first, then local profile file\\n  - default_scopes() returns [\\\"filesystem:write\\\",\\\"project:write\\\",\\\"memory:write\\\"]\\n  - default_expiry_seconds() returns 300 (5 minutes)\\n- Secret handling\\n  - Load secret from /home/wizard/broca/.token_auth/secret.key\\n  - Optional override via BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET environment variable\\n\\n3) Token guide and doc wiring\\n- BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md content should be copy-paste ready (draft in this chat can be used as a base)\\n- BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md: add a short section summarizing how to request/generate tokens and what constitutes “Approval” and “Actuator token provenance”\\n\\n4) Gate integration plan (non-destructive)\\n- In the actuator/write gate:\\n  - Import verify_token from broca.ops.tokens.token\\n  - If a valid token is presented with required scopes, allow the action\\n  - If invalid or missing, deny and return a concise plan for obtaining a token\\n  - In all allowed write actions, include an explicit “Approval” line and “Actuator token provenance” with token id (jti) and expiry\\n\\n5) Testing plan\\n- Unit tests for token.py:\\n  - generate_token -> verify_token succeeds\\n  - Expired token -> verification fails\\n  - Invalid signature -> verification fails\\n  - Missing/incorrect claims (iss/aud) -> failure\\n- Integration test sketch:\\n  - Simulate a write request with and without a valid token\\n  - Confirm gating behavior and provenance reporting\\n\\n6) Deployment rough timeline (phased)\\n- Phase 1: Documentation updates (quick)\\n- Phase 2: Skeleton code (read-only)\\n- Phase 3: Gate integration plan and tests (requires token)\\n- Phase 4: Pilot on a test write path with approval\\n\\nWhat I’ll provide next (ready-to-use)\\n- The exact content for:\\n  - broca/ops/tokens/BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md\\n  - broca/ops/tokens/__init__.py\\n  - broca/ops/tokens/token.py (skeleton)\\n  - broca/ops/tokens/defaults.py (skeleton)\\n  - broca/ops/tokens/cli.py (skeleton)\\n- A short patch outline to BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md describing the new workflow and provenance\\n\\nQuestions to lock in before I draft (quick yes/no)\\n- Secret key storage: keep /home/wizard/broca/.token_auth/secret.key (0600)?\\n- Env override: allow BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET as an override?\\n- Default scopes: keep as [\\\"filesystem:write\\\",\\\"project:write\\\",\\\"memory:write\\\"]? (or overrideable only via flag)\\n- Identity fallback detail: use git config name/email; if missing, fall back to \\\"Nick Yazdani\\\" with a safe sub \\\"nick.yazdani\\\"?\\nIf you approve, I’ll deliver the ready-to-paste skeletons and the updated docs in the next message.\"\n./conversations/37e91353-5a9a-451d-abc5-810286076acb.json:242:      \"content\": \"Here are the exact absolute paths to place everything, given /home/wizard/broca as the repo root and the code living under the Python package broca.\\n\\nCode (token subsystem)\\n- /home/wizard/broca/broca/ops/tokens/__init__.py\\n- /home/wizard/broca/broca/ops/tokens/token.py\\n- /home/wizard/broca/broca/ops/tokens/defaults.py\\n- /home/wizard/broca/broca/ops/tokens/cli.py\\n\\nDocumentation\\n- /home/wizard/broca/broca/ops/tokens/BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md\\n- (Documentation reference) /home/wizard/broca/BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md  [update to reference BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md]\\n\\nGate integration (where to hook token verification)\\n- Primary integration target (if you already have an actuator gate module): \\n  - /home/wizard/broca/broca/ops/gate/actuator_gate.py\\n- If your project does not yet have a gate module, create:\\n  - /home/wizard/broca/broca/ops/gate/__init__.py\\n  - /home/wizard/broca/broca/ops/gate/actuator_gate.py\\n- Note: In the gate, import verify_token from broca.ops.tokens.token and apply the token check on any non-read action. Ensure you add an explicit “Approval” line and include token provenance (jti, exp) in the response.\\n\\nTests (optional, recommended)\\n- /home/wizard/broca/tests/test_token_auth.py\\n- Place unit and integration tests here as you flesh out the implementation.\\n\\nEnvironment/secret handling\\n- Secret comes from environment or a .env as you specified:\\n  - BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET via environment (executes at runtime)\\n  - Optional .env support via broca/cli (your existing dotenv pattern)\\n\\nNotes\\n- Memory writes remain out of scope for now; gating and token should cover write actions except memory writes, which you’ll wire up on the next boot as discussed.\\n- You can tweak these paths later if your repo layout changes; for now this matches the current project structure as described.\\n\\nWould you like me to generate ready-to-paste file contents for these paths next (the skeletons and the BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md), so you can apply them directly?\"\n./conversations/37e91353-5a9a-451d-abc5-810286076acb.json:250:      \"content\": \"Yes. I’ve accounted for the two-repo setup and wired the token approval work to live in the code repo (BrocaOS) while keeping your house as the documentation/control layer.\\n\\nFactored layout (absolute paths)\\n\\nCode repo: /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS\\n- Token subsystem (core logic)\\n  - /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth/__init__.py\\n  - /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth/token.py\\n  - /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth/defaults.py\\n  - /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth/cli.py\\n- Gate integration (hook into existing escalation/gating)\\n  - /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/ops/gate/actuator_gate.py\\n    - Will import verify_token from broca.token_auth.token and enforce validation for non-read actions\\n- Documentation (canonical token docs live in code repo)\\n  - /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth/BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md\\n- Tests (recommended)\\n  - /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/tests/test_token_auth.py\\n\\nHouse repo: /home/wizard/broca\\n- Documentation layer and references\\n  - /home/wizard/broca/broca/ops/tokens/BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md\\n    - Readable summary that points to the code repo’s canonical BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md\\n  - /home/wizard/broca/BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md\\n    - Update to reference the token guide and the gating/provenance workflow\\n- Optional: a lightweight cross-link in house to the code repo’s token docs\\n\\nKey implications\\n- All actual token generation/verification logic and the gating hooks live in /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS.\\n- The house remains the control/documentation layer and provides high-level guidance, references, and provenance notes.\\n- Secret handling via environment (.env) remains supported by the code in BrocaOS; no secrets stored in “the house.”\\n\\nNext steps I can take (concise)\\n- Generate ready-to-paste skeletons for the BrocaOS token files (token.py, defaults.py, cli.py, __init__.py) and the BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md in /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS.\\n- Draft updated BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md in the house to reference the new token workflow and the gating/provenance requirements.\\n- Provide a minimal patch plan (diff-style) you can apply quickly.\\n\\nWould you like me to produce the ready-to-paste skeletons and the updated docs now? If yes, I’ll include:\\n- Code skeletons for token.py, defaults.py, cli.py, __init__.py\\n- BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md for the code repo\\n- A short patch/diff outline to update BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md in the house\"\n./conversations/37e91353-5a9a-451d-abc5-810286076acb.json:258:      \"content\": \"You're right—that was sloppy wording. I should have said the token work lives in the code repo, not “my house.” Here’s the clean mapping I’ll use from now on:\\n\\n- Code repo (where the token logic, gates, and tests live): /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS\\n  - Examples: broca/token_auth/, broca/ops/gate/, tests/, BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md (in the code repo)\\n- Documentation/control layer (the house): /home/wizard/broca\\n  - Examples: BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md, BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md (as references to the code repo), cross-links to code repo\\n\\nGoing forward I will refer to:\\n- code repo as the codebase or code repo\\n- the documentation/control layer as the docs room or docs repo\\n\\nWould you like me to proceed with generating the ready-to-paste skeletons and updated docs using this terminology?\"\n./conversations/37e91353-5a9a-451d-abc5-810286076acb.json:262:      \"content\": \"No you've misunderstood, YOU are BrocaOS, the code repo is: /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS and YOUR (BrocaOS, i.e. you the system) and your HOUSE (artifact store) refers to /home/wizard/broca, make sense?\"\n./conversations/37e91353-5a9a-451d-abc5-810286076acb.json:266:      \"content\": \"Understood. Final mapping for clarity:\\n\\n- Code repo (BrocaOS, where token logic and gates live): /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS\\n- House / artifact store (documentation/control layer): /home/wizard/broca\\n\\nExact absolute paths to place things (updated):\\n- Token subsystem (BrocaOS code repo)\\n  - /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth/__init__.py\\n  - /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth/token.py\\n  - /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth/defaults.py\\n  - /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth/cli.py\\n- Gate integration (BrocaOS code repo)\\n  - /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/ops/gate/actuator_gate.py\\n- Canonical docs for token (BrocaOS code repo)\\n  - /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth/BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md\\n- Tests (BrocaOS code repo)\\n  - /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/tests/test_token_auth.py\\n\\nHouse/docs (artifact/store) updates\\n- /home/wizard/broca/BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md\\n  - Reference the code-repo BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md and outline gating/provenance flow\\n- Do not duplicate the full token guide in the house; include a concise reference and links to the code repo for details\\n\\nWhat I’ll do next (concise)\\n- Produce ready-to-paste skeletons for the BrocaOS code paths above\\n- Draft minimal house-doc updates that reference the code repo\\n- Provide a compact patch/diff outline you can apply quickly\\n\\nConfirm: proceed with generating the skeletons and house-doc updates using this mapping?\"\n./conversations/37e91353-5a9a-451d-abc5-810286076acb.json:274:      \"content\": \"Acknowledged. Here are ready-to-paste skeletons and docs, aligned with the two-repo layout you confirmed:\\n\\nWhat I’m delivering\\n- Code repo: /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS\\n  - Token subsystem: broca/token_auth/*\\n  - Gate hook: broca/ops/gate/actuator_gate.py\\n  - Docs for token: broca/token_auth/BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md\\n  - Tests: tests/test_token_auth.py\\n- House/docs: /home/wizard/broca\\n  - BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md updated to reference code-repo token guide and gating\\n- All skeletons are read-only drafts ready to paste. They rely on environment-provided secrets (BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET or .env).\\n\\nCode repo skeletons (ready to paste)\\n\\n1) /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth/__init__.py\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nToken authorization package for BrocaOS (code repo).\\n\\nExports:\\n- generate_token, verify_token from broca.token_auth.token\\n- get_default_identity, default_scopes, default_expiry_seconds from defaults\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nfrom .token import generate_token, verify_token\\nfrom .defaults import get_default_identity, default_scopes, default_expiry_seconds  # convenience\\n\\n__all__ = [\\n    \\\"generate_token\\\",\\n    \\\"verify_token\\\",\\n    \\\"get_default_identity\\\",\\n    \\\"default_scopes\\\",\\n    \\\"default_expiry_seconds\\\",\\n]\\n\\n2) /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth/token.py\\nimport base64\\nimport json\\nimport time\\nimport uuid\\nimport hmac\\nimport hashlib\\nimport os\\n\\ndef _b64url(data: bytes) -> str:\\n    return base64.urlsafe_b64encode(data).rstrip(b\\\"=\\\").decode(\\\"ascii\\\")\\n\\ndef _b64url_decode(s: str) -> bytes:\\n    padding = \\\"=\\\" * ((4 - len(s) % 4) % 4)\\n    return base64.urlsafe_b64decode(s + padding)\\n\\ndef generate_token(sub: str, name: str, scopes, expiry_seconds: int, secret_key: str,\\n                   iss: str = \\\"broca-token-v1\\\", aud: str = \\\"broca-os\\\"):\\n    if not secret_key:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Secret key is required for token generation (BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET or .env).\\\")\\n    if isinstance(scopes, str):\\n        scopes_list = [s.strip() for s in scopes.split(\\\",\\\") if s.strip()]\\n    else:\\n        scopes_list = list(scopes)\\n\\n    iat = int(time.time())\\n    exp = int(iat + int(expiry_seconds))\\n    jti = uuid.uuid4().hex\\n\\n    header = {\\\"alg\\\": \\\"HS256\\\", \\\"typ\\\": \\\"JWT\\\"}\\n    payload = {\\n        \\\"sub\\\": sub,\\n        \\\"name\\\": name,\\n        \\\"scopes\\\": scopes_list,\\n        \\\"iat\\\": iat,\\n        \\\"exp\\\": exp,\\n        \\\"jti\\\": jti,\\n        \\\"iss\\\": iss,\\n        \\\"aud\\\": aud,\\n    }\\n\\n    header_b64 = _b64url(json.dumps(header, separators=(\\\",\\\", \\\":\\\"), sort_keys=True).encode(\\\"utf-8\\\"))\\n    payload_b64 = _b64url(json.dumps(payload, separators=(\\\",\\\", \\\":\\\"), sort_keys=True).encode(\\\"utf-8\\\"))\\n    signing_input = f\\\"{header_b64}.{payload_b64}\\\".encode(\\\"ascii\\\")\\n    signature = hmac.new(secret_key.encode(\\\"utf-8\\\"), signing_input, hashlib.sha256).digest()\\n    signature_b64 = _b64url(signature)\\n\\n    token = f\\\"{header_b64}.{payload_b64}.{signature_b64}\\\"\\n    return token, payload\\n\\ndef verify_token(token: str, secret_key: str, iss: str = \\\"broca-token-v1\\\", aud: str = \\\"broca-os\\\"):\\n    if not secret_key:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Secret key is required for token verification (BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET or .env).\\\")\\n\\n    parts = token.split(\\\".\\\")\\n    if len(parts) != 3:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid token format\\\")\\n\\n    header_b64, payload_b64, signature_b64 = parts\\n    signing_input = f\\\"{header_b64}.{payload_b64}\\\".encode(\\\"ascii\\\")\\n    expected_sig = hmac.new(secret_key.encode(\\\"utf-8\\\"), signing_input, hashlib.sha256).digest()\\n    expected_sig_b64 = _b64url(expected_sig)\\n\\n    if not hmac.compare_digest(expected_sig_b64, signature_b64):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid token signature\\\")\\n\\n    header = json.loads(_b64url_decode(header_b64).decode(\\\"utf-8\\\"))\\n    payload = json.loads(_b64url_decode(payload_b64).decode(\\\"utf-8\\\"))\\n\\n    if header.get(\\\"alg\\\") != \\\"HS256\\\":\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Unsupported algorithm\\\")\\n\\n    if payload.get(\\\"iss\\\") != iss or payload.get(\\\"aud\\\") != aud:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid issuer/audience\\\")\\n\\n    now = int(time.time())\\n    if int(payload.get(\\\"exp\\\", 0)) <= now:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Token has expired\\\")\\n\\n    return payload\\n\\n3) /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth/defaults.py\\nimport subprocess\\nimport json\\nimport os\\n\\ndef _git_config(key: str):\\n    try:\\n        out = subprocess.check_output([\\\"git\\\", \\\"config\\\", key], stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)\\n        return out.decode(\\\"utf-8\\\").strip()\\n    except Exception:\\n        return None\\n\\ndef _load_profile_file(path: str):\\n    if not os.path.exists(path):\\n        return None\\n    try:\\n        with open(path, \\\"r\\\", encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\") as f:\\n            data = json.load(f)\\n            return data\\n    except Exception:\\n        return None\\n\\ndef get_default_identity():\\n    # Git config first\\n    name = _git_config(\\\"user.name\\\")\\n    email = _git_config(\\\"user.email\\\")\\n\\n    if name:\\n        sub = name.lower().replace(\\\" \\\", \\\".\\\")\\n        return {\\\"sub\\\": sub, \\\"name\\\": name, \\\"email\\\": email or \\\"\\\"}\\n    # Fallback to profile file (if present)\\n    profile = _load_profile_file(\\\"BROCA_PROFILE.json\\\")\\n    if profile:\\n        sub = profile.get(\\\"sub\\\", \\\"nick.yazdani\\\")\\n        name = profile.get(\\\"name\\\", \\\"Nick Yazdani\\\")\\n        email = profile.get(\\\"email\\\", \\\"\\\")\\n        return {\\\"sub\\\": sub, \\\"name\\\": name, \\\"email\\\": email}\\n    # Final fallback\\n    return {\\\"sub\\\": \\\"nick.yazdani\\\", \\\"name\\\": \\\"Nick Yazdani\\\", \\\"email\\\": \\\"\\\"}\\n\\ndef default_scopes():\\n    return [\\\"filesystem:write\\\", \\\"project:write\\\", \\\"memory:write\\\"]\\n\\ndef default_expiry_seconds():\\n    # 5 minutes (per latest plan)\\n    return 300\\n\\n4) /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth/cli.py\\nimport argparse\\nimport json\\nimport os\\nimport time\\nfrom . import token as token_mod\\nfrom .defaults import get_default_identity, default_scopes, default_expiry_seconds\\n\\ndef _load_dotenv():\\n    dotenv_path = \\\".env\\\"\\n    if not os.path.exists(dotenv_path):\\n        return\\n    with open(dotenv_path, \\\"r\\\", encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\") as f:\\n        for line in f:\\n            line = line.strip()\\n            if not line or line.startswith(\\\"#\\\") or \\\"=\\\" not in line:\\n                continue\\n            k, v = line.split(\\\"=\\\", 1)\\n            if k not in os.environ:\\n                os.environ[k] = v\\n\\ndef main():\\n    _load_dotenv()\\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog=\\\"broca-token\\\", add_help=True)\\n    sub = parser.add_subparsers(dest=\\\"command\\\", required=True)\\n\\n    gen = sub.add_parser(\\\"generate\\\", help=\\\"Generate a JWT-like token with defaults\\\")\\n    gen.add_argument(\\\"--name\\\", help=\\\"Display name for the token (default autofill)\\\")\\n    gen.add_argument(\\\"--sub\\\", help=\\\"Subject identifier (default autofill)\\\")\\n    gen.add_argument(\\\"--scopes\\\", help=\\\"Comma-separated scopes (default autofill)\\\")\\n    gen.add_argument(\\\"--expiry-seconds\\\", type=int, help=\\\"Expiry in seconds (default 300)\\\")\\n    gen.add_argument(\\\"--expiry-iso\\\", help=\\\"Expiry as ISO timestamp (optional)\\\")\\n    gen.add_argument(\\\"--purpose\\\", help=\\\"Optional purpose text\\\")\\n\\n    args = parser.parse_args()\\n\\n    if args.command != \\\"generate\\\":\\n        print(\\\"Only 'generate' is implemented in this skeleton.\\\", flush=True)\\n        return\\n\\n    identity = get_default_identity()\\n    sub = args.sub or identity.get(\\\"sub\\\")\\n    name = args.name or identity.get(\\\"name\\\", \\\"\\\")\\n    secret_key = os.environ.get(\\\"BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET\\\")\\n\\n    if not secret_key:\\n        # Try to load from .env (already loaded into env by _load_dotenv)\\n        secret_key = os.environ.get(\\\"BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET\\\")\\n\\n    scopes = []\\n    if args.scopes:\\n        scopes = [s.strip() for s in args.scopes.split(\\\",\\\") if s.strip()]\\n    else:\\n        scopes = default_scopes()\\n\\n    expiry_seconds = args.expiry_seconds if args.expiry_seconds is not None else default_expiry_seconds()\\n\\n    token_str, payload = token_mod.generate_token(\\n        sub=sub,\\n        name=name,\\n        scopes=scopes,\\n        expiry_seconds=expiry_seconds,\\n        secret_key=secret_key if secret_key else \\\"\\\",\\n        iss=\\\"broca-token-v1\\\",\\n        aud=\\\"broca-os\\\",\\n    )\\n\\n    output = {\\\"token\\\": token_str, \\\"payload\\\": payload}\\n    print(json.dumps(output, indent=2))\\n\\n\\nif __name__ == \\\"__main__\\\":\\n    main()\\n\\n5) /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/ops/gate/actuator_gate.py\\nimport os\\nfrom broca.token_auth import verify_token\\n\\ndef authorize_action(action, token=None, required_scopes=None, secret_key=None, iss=\\\"broca-token-v1\\\", aud=\\\"broca-os\\\"):\\n    # Simple read-path exemption\\n    read_actions = {\\\"read\\\", \\\"read_file\\\", \\\"read_memory\\\"}\\n    if action in read_actions or (isinstance(action, str) and action.startswith(\\\"read\\\")):\\n        return True, \\\"Read action allowed without token\\\"\\n\\n    if not token:\\n        return False, \\\"Missing authorization token for write/action\\\"\\n\\n    if not secret_key:\\n        secret_key = os.environ.get(\\\"BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET\\\")\\n\\n    try:\\n        payload = verify_token(token, secret_key, iss=iss, aud=aud)\\n    except Exception as e:\\n        return False, f\\\"Token invalid: {str(e)}\\\"\\n\\n    scopes = payload.get(\\\"scopes\\\", [])\\n    if not required_scopes:\\n        required_scopes = []\\n\\n    # Ensure all required scopes are present\\n    if all(rs in scopes for rs in required_scopes):\\n        return True, f\\\"Approved (jti={payload.get('jti')}, exp={payload.get('exp')})\\\"\\n    else:\\n        return False, \\\"Insufficient token scopes for requested action\\\"\\n\\nNote: This is a minimal gate touchpoint intended for integration; adapt to your actual actuator gate call sites.\\n\\n6) /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth/BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md\\nBROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md\\n- Purpose\\n  - Document token issuance/validation, interoperable with BrocaOS gating.\\n- Token format\\n  - JWT-like, HS256 (HMAC-SHA256)\\n  - Structure: header.payload.signature (base64url, no padding)\\n- Claims (payload)\\n  - sub, name, scopes, iat, exp, jti, iss, aud\\n- Autofill (identity)\\n  - Git config user.name/user.email first; fallback to BROCA_PROFILE.json if present\\n- Secrets\\n  - BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET via environment or .env (not stored in-house)\\n- CLI\\n  - broca-token generate with sane defaults\\n- Validation flow\\n  - Gate uses verify_token(secret_key) and checks scopes\\n  - Outputs include an “Approval” line and token provenance (jti, exp)\\n- Security/audit\\n  - Short-lived tokens; logs for issuance and use\\n- References to next steps\\n  - Integrate with gating, add tests, add rotation plan\\n\\nTests skeleton (optional; ready-to-paste)\\n\\n7) /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/tests/test_token_auth.py\\nimport os\\nimport time\\nimport pytest\\nfrom broca.token_auth.token import generate_token, verify_token\\nfrom broca.token_auth.defaults import get_default_identity\\n\\ndef test_token_roundtrip(monkeypatch):\\n    monkeypatch.setenv(\\\"BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET\\\", \\\"supersecret123\\\")\\n    token, payload = generate_token(\\n        sub=\\\"nick.yazdani\\\",\\n        name=\\\"Nick Yazdani\\\",\\n        scopes=[\\\"filesystem:write\\\",\\\"project:write\\\",\\\"memory:write\\\"],\\n        expiry_seconds=5,\\n        secret_key=\\\"supersecret123\\\",\\n    )\\n    assert \\\"sub\\\" in payload and payload[\\\"sub\\\"] == \\\"nick.yazdani\\\"\\n    verified = verify_token(token, \\\"supersecret123\\\")\\n    assert verified[\\\"sub\\\"] == \\\"nick.yazdani\\\"\\n\\ndef test_token_expired(monkeypatch):\\n    monkeypatch.setenv(\\\"BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET\\\", \\\"supersecret123\\\")\\n    token, payload = generate_token(\\n        sub=\\\"nick.yazdani\\\",\\n        name=\\\"Nick Yazdani\\\",\\n        scopes=[\\\"filesystem:write\\\"],\\n        expiry_seconds=1,\\n        secret_key=\\\"supersecret123\\\",\\n    )\\n    time.sleep(2)\\n    import pytest\\n    with pytest.raises(ValueError):\\n        verify_token(token, \\\"supersecret123\\\")\\n\\ndef test_invalid_signature(monkeypatch):\\n    monkeypatch.setenv(\\\"BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET\\\", \\\"supersecret123\\\")\\n    token, payload = generate_token(\\n        sub=\\\"nick.yazdani\\\",\\n        name=\\\"Nick Yazdani\\\",\\n        scopes=[\\\"filesystem:write\\\"],\\n        expiry_seconds=10,\\n        secret_key=\\\"supersecret123\\\",\\n    )\\n    tampered = token[:-1] + (\\\"A\\\" if token[-1] != \\\"A\\\" else \\\"B\\\")\\n    with pytest.raises(ValueError):\\n        verify_token(tampered, \\\"supersecret123\\\")\\n\\ndef test_wrong_issuer_aud(monkeypatch):\\n    monkeypatch.setenv(\\\"BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET\\\", \\\"supersecret123\\\")\\n    token, payload = generate_token(\\n        sub=\\\"nick.yazdani\\\",\\n        name=\\\"Nick Yazdani\\\",\\n        scopes=[\\\"filesystem:write\\\"],\\n        expiry_seconds=10,\\n        secret_key=\\\"supersecret123\\\",\\n        iss=\\\"broca-token-v1\\\",\\n        aud=\\\"broca-os\\\",\\n    )\\n    # verify with wrong issuer\\n    with pytest.raises(ValueError):\\n        verify_token(token, \\\"supersecret123\\\", iss=\\\"wrong-iss\\\", aud=\\\"broca-os\\\")\\n\\nHow to integrate (summary)\\n- Put these skeletons into the exact paths above.\\n- Update the house doc BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md to reference:\\n  - The code repo path for token logic: /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS\\n  - The token guide: BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md in the code repo\\n  - Gate/provenance workflow\\n- When ready to actually write, you’ll need operator approval and an actuator token per your policy.\\n\\nHouse documentation update (concise)\\n\\n- /home/wizard/broca/BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md\\n  - Add a short section “Token-based approvals (BrocaOS)” that points to:\\n    - Code repo: /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth/BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md\\n  - Summarize gating/provenance requirements:\\n    - Any non-read action requires a valid token and an explicit Approval line plus token provenance (jti, exp)\\n  - Note about memory-write caveat: pointer-sync on next boot; gating will cover other writes now\\n- You’ll have a cross-link: house doc references code repo for implementation details and tests.\\n\\nNext steps\\n- If you want, I can paste patches as a diff-like outline, or provide a single bundle you can copy-paste into your editor to create files.\\n- Tell me if you want any tweaks to the defaults, or to the gate logic (e.g., how strictly scopes are checked, or how many write paths to gate first).\\n\\nWould you like me to proceed by also providing a compact “diff patch” block you can apply, or is the per-file paste sufficient for you?\"\n./conversations/37e91353-5a9a-451d-abc5-810286076acb.json:282:      \"content\": \"Yes. Here is a complete, self-contained insertion you can drop into BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md. It’s scoped to your two-repo setup and uses the terminology we settled on.\\n\\nText to insert (full block)\\n\\n### Token-based approvals (BrocaOS)\\n\\nOverview\\n- BrocaOS uses short-lived JWT-like tokens (HS256) to gate non-read actions. Tokens are issued from the code repository and validated by the existing gate/escalation system.\\n- The canonical implementation lives in the code repo: /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth\\n- The house serves as the documentation/control layer and should reference the code repo for details and testing.\\n\\nToken issuance and validation (where to find the logic)\\n- Token logic (generation and verification) lives at:\\n  - /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth/token.py\\n  - /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth/defaults.py\\n  - /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth/cli.py\\n- A minimal interface is exposed via broca.token_auth (importable as needed in gates):\\n  - generate_token(sub, name, scopes, expiry_seconds, secret_key, iss=\\\"broca-token-v1\\\", aud=\\\"broca-os\\\")\\n  - verify_token(token, secret_key, iss=\\\"broca-token-v1\\\", aud=\\\"broca-os\\\")\\n- The code uses a secret key sourced from the environment (BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET) or a .env file. It is not stored in the house.\\n\\nIdentity autofill and defaults\\n- Identity autofill order:\\n  - Primary: git config user.name and user.email\\n  - Fallback: local profile file BROCA_PROFILE.json in the code repo (if present)\\n  - Final fallback: sub/names set to nick.yazdani / Nick Yazdani\\n- Default scopes (unless overridden by explicit input) are:\\n  - [\\\"filesystem:write\\\", \\\"project:write\\\", \\\"memory:write\\\"]\\n- Token expiry default\\n  - 5 minutes (300 seconds)\\n\\nToken usage and gating workflow\\n- When a non-read action is requested, a valid token must be provided that includes all required scopes for that action.\\n- Gate integration (example): verify_token(token, secret_key) is invoked; if valid and scopes match, action proceeds.\\n- Outputs must include:\\n  - An explicit “Approval” line indicating token-based authorization\\n  - Token provenance including the token’s jti and exp (for audit)\\n- Memory writes caveat\\n  - Memory-write paths are not yet wired; they will be synchronized on the next boot as discussed. Gate the rest of the write paths now.\\n\\nSecrets and rotation\\n- Secret handling is external to the house: use BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET in the environment or a .env file loaded at runtime.\\n- Rotation plan should be documented in BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md (in code repo) and mirrored in a house reference:\\n  - Keep a changelog of secret rotations and revoke old tokens as needed.\\n\\nPaths to references (for quick navigation)\\n- Canonical token guide in code repo: /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth/BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md\\n- Gate/EScalation integration: /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/ops/gate/actuator_gate.py\\n- Code repo roots for token tooling: /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth\\n- House reference pointing to code repo: /home/wizard/broca/BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md\\n\\nWhat to do next (concise)\\n- Append this block to the end of BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md in /home/wizard/broca.\\n- Optionally add a short cross-link in the house to the code repo’s BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md:\\n  - “Token-based approvals (BrocaOS): see code repo at /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth/BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md for implementation details.”\\n- If you want, I can generate a compact patch/diff you can apply in one go.\\n\\nLine-number guidance\\n- Exact line numbers depend on your current BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md content. A safe approach is:\\n  - Append the block to the end of BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md.\\n  - If you want an explicit line number, first determine the current line count of BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md (e.g., via wc -l BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md). Then insert starting at line (current_line_count + 1).\\n- If you share the present tail of BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md (the last 50–100 lines), I’ll give you an exact insertion point with a precise line number.\\n\\nWould you like me to also provide a ready-to-paste house-side reference snippet (one paragraph) that links to the code repo, or is the above block sufficient? If you want, I can generate a diff patch as well for immediate application.\"\n./conversations/37e91353-5a9a-451d-abc5-810286076acb.json:290:      \"content\": \"Great. Here’s a concise, action-focused integration plan with concrete steps you can execute now. It keeps the token logic in the code repo and wires gating into your existing actuator flow.\\n\\nConcise integration plan (one-page)\\n- Target: BrocaOS code repo at /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS\\n- Gate hook to update: broca/ops/gate/actuator_gate.py\\n- Token module: broca/token_auth (token.py, defaults.py, cli.py, __init__.py)\\n- Documentation: BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md already in code repo; cross-link in BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md (house)\\n- Environment: BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET supplied via environment or .env (do not store secrets in the house)\\n\\nWhat to implement now (exact actions)\\n1) Gate integration change (in /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/ops/gate/actuator_gate.py)\\n- Add import:\\n  from broca.token_auth import verify_token\\n- Update authorization flow to gate non-read actions by tokens:\\n  - If action is a read-type, allow without token (still document this in code comments).\\n  - If token is missing or invalid, return an error with a concise plan for obtaining a valid token.\\n  - If token valid, ensure required_scopes are present; on success, return an explicit approval message including the token’s jti and exp for audit.\\n- Exact patch to apply (diff-like patch you can paste and apply):\\n\\n*** Begin Patch\\n*** Update File: broca/ops/gate/actuator_gate.py\\n+import os\\n+from broca.token_auth import verify_token\\n+\\n+def authorize_action(action, token=None, required_scopes=None, secret_key=None, iss=\\\"broca-token-v1\\\", aud=\\\"broca-os\\\"):\\n+    # Read paths are allowed without a token (adjust as needed for your policy)\\n+    read_actions = {\\\"read\\\", \\\"read_file\\\", \\\"read_memory\\\"}\\n+    if action in read_actions or (isinstance(action, str) and action.startswith(\\\"read\\\")):\\n+        return True, \\\"Read action allowed without token\\\"\\n+\\n+    if not token:\\n+        return False, \\\"Missing authorization token for write/action\\\"\\n+\\n+    if not secret_key:\\n+        secret_key = os.environ.get(\\\"BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET\\\")\\n+\\n+    try:\\n+        payload = verify_token(token, secret_key, iss=iss, aud=aud)\\n+    except Exception as e:\\n+        return False, f\\\"Token invalid: {str(e)}\\\"\\n+\\n+    scopes = payload.get(\\\"scopes\\\", [])\\n+    if not required_scopes:\\n+        required_scopes = []\\n+\\n+    # Ensure all required scopes are present\\n+    if all(rs in scopes for rs in required_scopes):\\n+        return True, f\\\"Approved (jti={payload.get('jti')}, exp={payload.get('exp')})\\\"\\n+    else:\\n+        return False, \\\"Insufficient token scopes for requested action\\\"\\n*** End Patch\\n\\nNotes:\\n- This integrates with the existing gate logic. The caller should, on a successful return, emit an explicit “Approval” line and a token provenance block (jti/exp) in the user-visible output as part of your standard response template.\\n\\n2) Ensure token generation/verification paths exist and are imported\\n- Confirm:\\n  - /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth/token.py provides generate_token and verify_token (as sketched).\\n  - /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth/defaults.py provides get_default_identity, default_scopes, default_expiry_seconds.\\n  - /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth/cli.py provides a generate command with autofill behavior.\\n- If any of these files are missing or differ, align them with the skeletons I previously provided.\\n\\n3) House (docs) alignment\\n- In /home/wizard/broca/BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md, add a short integration note (concise):\\n  - Token-based approvals (BrocaOS) now gate non-read actions via tokens produced in /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth.\\n  - Gate behavior: verify_token validates the JWT-like token with BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET (env/.env). On success, actions proceed; outputs must include an explicit Approval line and token provenance (jti, exp).\\n  - Memory-write path remains out-of-scope for now; pointer synchronization will occur on the next boot.\\n  - Cross-link to the code repo for implementation details: /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/broca/token_auth/BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md\\n- Optional house cross-link: add a one-line pointer near the top of BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md to the code repo for the token guide.\\n\\n4) Testing plan (quick)\\n- Unit test: tests/test_token_auth.py already sketched; ensure tests cover:\\n  - Token generation + verification\\n  - Expired token raises\\n  - Invalid signature raises\\n  - Wrong issuer/aud raises\\n- Gate test: add tests/test_gate_integration.py to simulate authorize_action with:\\n  - Read action (token not required)\\n  - Write action with valid token and required scopes\\n  - Write action with missing token\\n  - Write action with token lacking required scopes\\n- Run tests in the code repo:\\n  - cd /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS\\n  - pytest -q\\n\\n5) Operational rollout (minimal risk)\\n- Deploy changes to code repo only (BrocaOS).\\n- Ensure environment variable BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET is set in your runtime environments (or provide a .env at runtime; the CLI already supports .env).\\n- Validate by running the generator:\\n  - broca-token generate (from the code repo’s tooling)\\n  - Use the produced token to call authorize_action through your gate path (or a quick integration script) to confirm gating works end-to-end.\\n- Update house docs with a tiny “how to test” checklist for operators.\\n\\nWhat to send me next (quick)\\n- If you want, paste the current contents of:\\n  - broca/ops/gate/actuator_gate.py (so I can tailor the patch to your actual function names)\\n  - BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md tail (to place an exact insertion point for the new section)\\n- I can generate an exact final patch (diff) with line anchors for your files, plus a short test harness you can drop in.\\n\\nOptional quick-start commands (copy-paste)\\n- Apply the patch above (assuming you’re in the code repo root):\\n  - cat > /tmp/integration.patch << 'PATCH'\\n  (paste the patch block from above)\\n  PATCH\\n  - git apply /tmp/integration.patch\\n  - rm /tmp/integration.patch\\n- Run tests:\\n  - pytest -q\\n- Run the token generator to sanity-check environment:\\n  - broca-token generate\\n\\nValidation checklist (before you run)\\n- BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET is available in the environment (or .env is loaded by your runtime)\\n- The actuator gate path is updated to import verify_token (as shown)\\n- Read actions bypass token; write actions require token with proper scopes\\n\\nWould you like me to tailor the patch to your exact existing actuator_gate.py content (so the insertion points and function signatures align perfectly), or is the generic patch sufficient for you to apply now?\"\n./conversations/37e91353-5a9a-451d-abc5-810286076acb.json:306:      \"content\": \"Here’s a thorough, actionable answer to both questions.\\n\\nPart A) What BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET should contain\\n\\n- What it is\\n  - A cryptographically strong shared secret used to sign and verify HS256 (HMAC-SHA256) JWT-like tokens.\\n  - It must be secret (not checked into source control) and kept only in environment context.\\n\\n- What it should look like\\n  - A random 256-bit (32-byte) value, represented as hex or base64. Either is fine as long as your verifier uses the same representation consistently.\\n\\n- Recommended formats (pick one)\\n  - Hex: 64 hex characters (32 bytes)\\n  - Base64: 44 characters (approximately) after URL-safe encoding\\n\\n- Examples (for illustration only; replace with a real secret)\\n  - Hex example: 9f2a8e4b7c1d3f2a9a7e6b4c1d2e3f405162738495a1b2c3d4e5f60718293a4\\n  - Base64 example (not to be used as-is): ZG9ja2VzdG9yZW5jcnVpdGUtY2hhbm5lbmJ1dGVzLWZvci1sb2NhdGlvbg\\n\\n- How to create a real secret (recommended)\\n  - Python: python - << 'PY'\\n    import secrets\\n    print(secrets.token_hex(32))\\n    PY\\n  - Bash: head -c 32 /dev/urandom | xxd -p -c 32\\n  - OpenSSL: openssl rand -hex 32\\n\\n- How to store it safely\\n  - Do not commit to any repo.\\n  - Use environment-based storage:\\n    - Set in the environment at runtime: export BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET=\\\"YOUR_GENERATED_VALUE\\\"\\n    - Or place in a .env file at the code repo root (the code reads .env in the BrocaOS code path):\\n      BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET=YOUR_GENERATED_VALUE\\n  - In production, prefer a secure secret store or a container/k8s secret mechanism rather than a plain file.\\n\\n- Important operational notes\\n  - Rotation: have a plan to rotate the secret periodically and gracefully revoke old tokens (e.g., by expiring tokens or maintaining a short revocation list).\\n  - If BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET leaks, invalidate tokens by rotating the secret and reissuing them only to trusted clients.\\n\\nPart B) Thorough integration analysis and plan (what’s left and how to finish)\\n\\n1) Current state recap (two-repo setup)\\n- Code repo (BrocaOS): /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS\\n  - Contains token logic (broca/token_auth), gate integration, and tests skeletons\\n  - BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md lives here as the canonical token guide\\n  - actuator_gate.py now wired to verify_token via broca.token_auth\\n- House/docs repo (artifact store): /home/wizard/broca\\n  - BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md updated to reference the code repo’s token guide\\n  - Serves as the control layer for governance, not the implementation\\n\\n2) Architecture and data flow\\n- Token production\\n  - broca/token_auth/cli.py (and __init__.py) expose a generate command\\n  - Identity autofill uses git config first, then BROCA_PROFILE.json (if present)\\n  - Default scopes: filesystem:write, project:write, memory:write\\n  - Expiry: 5 minutes by default\\n  - Secret security: BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET from env/.env at runtime\\n- Token contents\\n  - JWT-like structure with HS256 signature\\n  - Payload includes sub, name, scopes, iat, exp, jti, iss, aud\\n- Gate path\\n  - actuator_gate.py uses verify_token(token, secret_key) and checks required scopes\\n  - Read actions bypass token\\n  - Writes require token and correct scopes\\n  - On success, gate returns an explicit approval message including jti/exp for audit\\n- Memory writes\\n  - Out-of-scope for immediate integration; will rely on next-boot pointer synchronization\\n  - Gate remains functional for all other write paths\\n\\n3) What remains to implement (concrete, prioritized)\\n- Complete code integration in BrocaOS\\n  - Ensure all token modules exist and are importable:\\n    - broca/token_auth/__init__.py, token.py, defaults.py, cli.py\\n  - Gate: actuator_gate.py patch applied and tested with sample tokens\\n- Documentation alignment\\n  - Finalize BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md in code repo (already drafted)\\n  - Update house BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md with explicit “how to test” steps and links\\n- Tests\\n  - Implement tests/test_token_auth.py (unit tests for generate_token/verify_token)\\n  - Implement tests/test_gate_integration.py (gate authorization scenarios)\\n  - Run via pytest in /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS\\n- Deployment and runtime\\n  - Ensure BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET is supplied in runtime environments\\n  - Ensure the .env loader is compatible with your runtime (you already have a .env loader in the CLI)\\n- Security hardening\\n  - Review token signing and verification for edge cases\\n  - Ensure tokens are not logged/printed in plain form\\n  - Consider adding a minimal revocation mechanism (optional) or at least rotation plan\\n- Memory writes integration plan\\n  - Design a pointer-sync mechanism to apply queued memory writes on next boot\\n  - Gate memory write paths with a separate scope or a dedicated flag, so you can enable after boot\\n\\n4) Testing and validation plan (step-by-step)\\n- Unit tests\\n  - Token generation + verification round-trip\\n  - Expiry enforcement\\n  - Signature invalidation\\n  - Issuer/audience validation\\n- Gate integration tests\\n  - Read action allowed without token\\n  - Write action with valid token and required scopes passes\\n  - Write action without token is rejected\\n  - Write action with token missing required scopes is rejected\\n- End-to-end sanity test\\n  - Generate a token with broca-token generate\\n  - Use that token to simulate an actuator action gate path (through actuator_gate.authorize_action)\\n  - Confirm output includes an Approval line and provenance (jti/exp)\\n\\n5) Operational rollout plan (low risk)\\n- Start in code repo only\\n  - Set BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET in your environment\\n  - Run the token CLI to generate tokens\\n  - Simulate gating with actuator_gate\\n  - Validate Outputs contain the expected Approval and provenance lines\\n- Move to house docs\\n  - Ensure operators understand the gating flow and token provenance\\n  - Provide a simple runbook for token generation and gate validation\\n- Plan for memory writes\\n  - Prepare the pointer-sync plan for the next boot and update gating accordingly\\n\\n6) Security considerations (critical)\\n- Secret handling\\n  - Never hardcode or store secrets in code or docs\\n  - Use environment variables or a secure .env at runtime\\n- Token TTL\\n  - 5 minutes minimizes risk; for high-security environments you might reduce TTL further or implement a token-reuse policy with nonce (jti) checks\\n- Logging\\n  - Do not log full tokens; log token provenance (jti, exp) and action path\\n- Revocation and rotation\\n  - Implement a rotation strategy for BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET and a simple revocation checklist for compromised tokens\\n- Transport and auditing\\n  - Ensure any token transmission happens over secure channels (TLS in actual deployments)\\n\\n7) Quick-start commands you can use now (compact)\\n- Generate a new secret (example; replace with actual generated value)\\n  - SECRET=$(python -c \\\"import secrets; print(secrets.token_hex(32))\\\")\\n  - export BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET=\\\"$SECRET\\\"\\n- Or in a .env file:\\n  - BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET=\\\"$SECRET\\\"\\n- Generate a token (would print in code repo)\\n  - broca-token generate\\n- Validate via gate snippet (pseudo)\\n  - token=$(echo \\\"<TOKEN_FROM_PREVIOUS_COMMAND>\\\" | tr -d '\\\\n')\\n  - python -c \\\"from broca.token_auth.token import verify_token; print(verify_token('$token', '$SECRET'))\\\"\\n\\n8) What I need from you to finish cleanly\\n- Confirm you want me to:\\n  - Provide a final diff patch that you can apply in one go, along with a short runbook\\n  - Or provide ready-to-paste full-file contents for actuator_gate.py plus a small test harness\\n- If you want tests included in the repo, confirm whether to add pytest configuration and any test dependencies\\n\\nIf you’d like, I can next produce:\\n- A precise, ready-to-paste patch for actuator_gate.py (already prepared in previous messages) plus ready-to-paste unit tests (test_token_auth.py and test_gate_integration.py)\\n- A compact diff block you can apply with git apply (including a small patch for BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md cross-link, if desired)\\n\\nWould you like me to generate the exact patch block for:\\n- The actuator_gate.py replacement (already provided), plus\\n- A new tests/test_token_auth.py and tests/test_gate_integration.py, plus\\n- The house-side BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md insertion snippet?\"\n./conversations/37e91353-5a9a-451d-abc5-810286076acb.json:314:      \"content\": \"Here’s a concise recap of the conversation so far (≤250 tokens):\\n\\n- Goals\\n  - Implement a BrocaOS token subsystem using HS256 JWT-like tokens with a default 5-minute expiry.\\n  - Add identity autofill (git config first, then local profile file).\\n  - Use BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET from environment (override via .env).\\n  - Provide a CLI: broca-token generate (auto-fill by default).\\n  - Gate non-read actions via verify_token; always include an Approval line and token provenance in outputs.\\n  - Keep memory writes out of scope for now; plan pointer sync next boot.\\n\\n- Architecture (two-repo setup)\\n  - Code repo: /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS\\n    - Token logic: broca/token_auth (init.py, token.py, defaults.py, cli.py)\\n    - Gate hook: broca/ops/gate/actuator_gate.py\\n    - Docs: BROCA_TOKEN_GUIDE.md (in code repo)\\n  - House/docs: /home/wizard/broca\\n    - BROCA_OPERATORS_GUIDE.md updated to reference code-repo token guide and gating\\n\\n- Key decisions\\n  - Token format: JWT-like HS256\\n  - Default expiry: 5 minutes\\n  - Identity autofill: git config -> local profile\\n  - Secret source: BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET (env); .env override\\n  - CLI: broca-token generate\\n  - Gate behavior: verify_token on actuator actions; include Approval line and provenance\\n  - Memory writes: deferred\\n\\n- Open items / next steps\\n  - Provide full code/diff for actuator_gate.py and token subsystem skeletons (paste-ready).\\n  - Complete integration: wiring verify_token into actuator flow, tests, and docs cross-links.\\n  - Confirm absolute paths and two-repo references in all docs.\"\n./conversations/37e91353-5a9a-451d-abc5-810286076acb.json:330:      \"content\": \"Here is a ready-to-run test harness you can drop in and execute to validate the integration so far. It exercises token generation, verification, and gate authorization without requiring any patching at runtime beyond what’s already in the code repo.\\n\\nWhat you’ll run\\n- Location: /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/tests/test_integration_harness.py\\n- Prereqs:\\n  - BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET must be set (env or .env) for token signing/verification\\n  - The codebase in /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS is the source of truth\\n- How to run:\\n  - Optionally load a .env if you use one (the script will try to read a local .env)\\n  - python3 /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/tests/test_integration_harness.py\\n\\nScript: test_integration_harness.py (paste this into the file)\\n\\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nIntegration harness for BrocaOS token flow (end-to-end test).\\n\\nTests:\\n- Token generation with defaults\\n- Token verification\\n- Gate authorization using the token (write path)\\n- Read path gating (no token required)\\n- Optional: small expiry test (short TTL)\\n\\nNotes:\\n- This is a self-contained harness that uses the code in /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS.\\n- It does not modify the house/docs; it exercises code-paths in the code repo.\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\nimport os\\nimport time\\nimport pathlib\\n\\n# Lightweight .env loader (no external deps)\\ndef _load_env_file(path=\\\".env\\\"):\\n    if not pathlib.Path(path).exists():\\n        return\\n    with open(path, \\\"r\\\", encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\") as f:\\n        for line in f:\\n            line = line.strip()\\n            if not line or line.startswith(\\\"#\\\") or \\\"=\\\" not in line:\\n                continue\\n            k, v = line.split(\\\"=\\\", 1)\\n            if k not in os.environ:\\n                os.environ[k] = v\\n\\n_load_env_file()  # optional; will set BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET if present in .env\\n\\n# Imports from the code repo\\nfrom broca.token_auth.token import generate_token, verify_token\\nfrom broca.token_auth.defaults import get_default_identity, default_scopes, default_expiry_seconds\\nfrom broca.ops.gate.actuator_gate import authorize_action\\n\\ndef main():\\n    secret = os.environ.get(\\\"BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET\\\")\\n    if not secret:\\n        print(\\\"ERROR: BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET not set. Export a secret via env or .env.\\\")\\n        print(\\\"Example: export BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET=$(python -c \\\\\\\"import secrets; print(secrets.token_hex(32))\\\\\\\")\\\")\\n        return 2\\n\\n    # Identity autofill (via defaults)\\n    identity = get_default_identity()\\n    sub = identity.get(\\\"sub\\\", \\\"nick.yazdani\\\")\\n    name = identity.get(\\\"name\\\", \\\"Nick Yazdani\\\")\\n\\n    scopes = default_scopes()\\n    expiry_seconds = default_expiry_seconds()\\n\\n    print(\\\"=== Token Generation (defaults) ===\\\")\\n    token, payload = generate_token(sub=sub, name=name, scopes=scopes, expiry_seconds=expiry_seconds, secret_key=secret, iss=\\\"broca-token-v1\\\", aud=\\\"broca-os\\\")\\n    print(\\\"Token:\\\", token)\\n    print(\\\"Payload:\\\", payload)\\n\\n    print(\\\"\\\\n=== Token Verification (sanity) ===\\\")\\n    verified = verify_token(token, secret, iss=\\\"broca-token-v1\\\", aud=\\\"broca-os\\\")\\n    print(\\\"Verified payload:\\\", verified)\\n\\n    print(\\\"\\\\n=== Gate Test: Non-read action with token (required scopes) ===\\\")\\n    action = \\\"write_config\\\"  # non-read example\\n    required_scopes = [\\\"filesystem:write\\\"]\\n    ok, msg = authorize_action(action, token=token, required_scopes=required_scopes, secret_key=secret)\\n    print(\\\"Authorization result:\\\", ok, \\\"| message:\\\", msg)\\n\\n    print(\\\"\\\\n=== Gate Test: Read action (no token required) ===\\\")\\n    ok_read, msg_read = authorize_action(\\\"read\\\", token=None, required_scopes=None)\\n    print(\\\"Read action result:\\\", ok_read, \\\"| message:\\\", msg_read)\\n\\n    print(\\\"\\\\n=== Optional Expiry Test (short TTL) ===\\\")\\n    # Demonstrates expiry behavior with a tiny TTL to avoid long waits\\n    short_ttl = 2  # seconds\\n    token2, payload2 = generate_token(sub=sub, name=name, scopes=scopes, expiry_seconds=short_ttl, secret_key=secret, iss=\\\"broca-token-v1\\\", aud=\\\"broca-os\\\")\\n    print(\\\"Generated short-TTL token exp:\\\", payload2.get(\\\"exp\\\"))\\n    time.sleep(3)\\n    try:\\n        verify_token(token2, secret, iss=\\\"broca-token-v1\\\", aud=\\\"broca-os\\\")\\n        print(\\\"Expiry test: FAILED (token should have expired)\\\")\\n    except Exception as e:\\n        print(\\\"Expiry test: PASSED (token expired as expected):\\\", str(e))\\n\\nif __name__ == \\\"__main__\\\":\\n    raise SystemExit(main())\\n\\nHow to run and what to expect\\n- Pre-flight:\\n  - Ensure you have a valid BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET in your environment\\n  - If you want to test .env, place BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET=value in a .env at /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS\\n- Run:\\n  - python3 /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS/tests/test_integration_harness.py\\n- Expected outcomes:\\n  - A generated token and its payload printed\\n  - verify_token returns the payload when using the same secret\\n  - Gate test with action requiring filesystem:write succeeds\\n  - Read path test returns allowed without token\\n  - Optional expiry test demonstrates token expiration\\n\\nConcise invariants to maintain (continuity essentials)\\n- Code repo is the source of truth for token logic and gating; house remains the control/document layer.\\n- SECRET handling\\n  - BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET must be provided via environment or a .env; never stored in the house/docs\\n  - If secret leaks, rotate it and reissue tokens\\n- Token model and lifecycle\\n  - JWT-like HS256 tokens, short-lived by default (5 minutes)\\n  - Payload fields: sub, name, scopes, iat, exp, jti, iss, aud\\n  - Signature computed with HMAC-SHA256 using BROCA_TOKEN_SECRET\\n- Autofill behavior\\n  - Identity comes from git config first, then BROCA_PROFILE.json, then safe defaults\\n- Gate semantics\\n  - Read actions bypass token\\n  - Non-read actions require a token with all required scopes\\n  - Successful gate outcomes must include an explicit Approval line and token provenance (jti, exp)\\n- Memory writes\\n  - Deferred to next boot (pointer synchronization)\\n  - Gate the rest of the writes now; memory path will be wired later\\n- Versioning and structure\\n  - Two repos in play:\\n    - Code repo: /home/wizard/Documents/Code/BrocaOS\\n    - House repo (docs/control): /home/wizard/broca\\n  - All cross-references in docs should point to the code repo for implementation details and tests\\n\\nIf you want, I can tailor this harness to reflect any special edge cases in your actual actuator_gate.py (e.g., different action names) or add a second harness that shells out to the broca-token CLI for token generation. Would you like me to add a CLI-based harness as well, or keep everything in Python for speed?\"\n./conversations/37e91353-5a9a-451d-abc5-810286076acb.json:338:      \"content\": \"Here’s a compact continuity packet you can carry into the next session. It fuses the invariants I must maintain with the current session’s